WEBVTT

00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:05.156
A service can handle normal traffic and
still fail when a dependency goes down.

00:00:05.327 --> 00:00:07.535
Replay the traffic with one dependency

00:00:07.535 --> 00:00:10.615
fault, then check what happens once the
fault clears.

00:00:11.185 --> 00:00:15.170
In the replay test config, scope the rule
to a mocked dependency.

00:00:15.341 --> 00:00:17.944
For example, give the payments mock a 503

00:00:17.944 --> 00:00:21.564
status, and use the route to target just
the charge call.

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:25.303
A faulted request and recovery are judged
separately.

00:00:25.474 --> 00:00:30.132
In a grading test, one order request passed
the payments error through to the caller.

00:00:30.303 --> 00:00:35.190
Later, the orders endpoint answered
successfully after the rule stopped firing.

00:00:35.752 --> 00:00:37.943
To measure recovery in one dashboard

00:00:37.943 --> 00:00:41.655
replay, set when the chaos rule starts and
how long it lasts.

00:00:41.826 --> 00:00:46.690
Keep sending requests to the affected
endpoint after the window closes so the

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.333
report can judge recovery.

00:00:48.885 --> 00:00:51.152
Open Readiness and choose Resilience.

00:00:51.323 --> 00:00:56.498
Check how many faulted requests the app
handled, then look for retry and latency

00:00:56.498 --> 00:00:58.956
amplification and the recovery result.

00:00:59.127 --> 00:01:01.746
The Mocks action takes you to the perturbed
calls.

00:01:02.285 --> 00:01:07.181
If recovery is unknown, check whether any
requests reached the affected endpoint

00:01:07.181 --> 00:01:08.527
after the fault ended.
